payload.message:
Yes, custom pivot models are the cleaner fix if we keep UUID `id` columns on pivot tables.

Right now `company_employee` and `employee_project` are not pure Laravel default pivots because they have their own required UUID primary key. A custom pivot model lets the relationship behave normally while the pivot model owns UUID generation.

Recommended direction:

- Add `CompanyEmployee` custom pivot model for `company_employee`
- Add `EmployeeProject` custom pivot model for `employee_project`
- Use `HasUuids` on both pivot models
- Add `->using(CompanyEmployee::class)` / `->using(EmployeeProject::class)` on relationships
- Then standard calls like `attach()`, `syncWithoutDetaching()`, and seeders can work without manually passing `id`

One caveat: Laravel pivot models usually need to extend `Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\Pivot`, not `Model`.

Shape:

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Concerns\HasUuids;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\Pivot;

class CompanyEmployee extends Pivot
{
    use HasUuids;

    public $incrementing = false;

    protected $keyType = 'string';
}
```

Then:

```php
return $this->belongsToMany(Employee::class)
    ->using(CompanyEmployee::class)
    ->withTimestamps();
```

Same idea for `EmployeeProject`, with `->withPivot('company_id')`.

Alternative would be removing the `id` from pivot tables and using composite uniqueness only. That is simpler for default Eloquent, but since we already chose UUID PKs consistently, custom pivot models fit the current schema better.

